design card:
id: r8-s-pf256-disp
parents:
  - r6-s-ldef-nc-disp
  - r7-ldg256-l2pf-mdisp
operation: specialisation
language: cuda_cpp
niche:
  mem: ldg256
  st: direct
  grid: oneshot
  launch: fused
  tile: rows16
  red: halfwarp
  cache: stream
  spec: dispatch:size
hypothesis: >-
  What the small-input fixed cost is made of. Measured on the rented B200 with harness CUPTI timing,
  cold L2 after the zero flush:
  (1) The CUPTI span of an empty kernel is 1.45-1.65 us at any grid size (1 to 1536 CTAs).
  (2) The first DRAM access costs about 0.6 us over that; each further dependent load costs about 0.45 us,
  with no extra cost for new 4 KB, 2 MB or 8 MB pages, so TLB misses do not matter.
  (3) Reading 6.3 MB (Q+K at 128 tokens, no stores) costs 2.4 us over an empty kernel, about 2.6 TB/s.
  Our reading: every read allocation evicts one of the zero flush's dirty L2 lines, so the read phase
  carries about the same volume of write-backs. Stores alone cost 0.9 us; weight loads about 1%.
  The parent (4.1 us at 128 tokens) already sits at launch span + first latency + dirty-doubled DRAM
  traffic. No kernel-side lever moved it: 64- to 1024-thread CTAs, 1-4 rows per thread, a balanced
  148xk interleaved mapping, discard of input lines, L2::256B prefetch size, L1::no_allocate, and
  dropping the weight loads all came within +-1% or were worse. The one repeatable gain is r7's L2 bulk
  prefetch one resident wave ahead: -2% at 256 tokens (1.3 waves), +1.7% at 586 tokens, a no-op at 128.
  So it applies only up to 300 tokens. 301-600 tokens run the parent's S path and >600 the parent's
  large path, both unchanged. Full test on the rented B200 against the parent: 16/16 pass;
  S -1.9% (256-token sizes -0.9 to -4.8%); M -0.8% and L -0.2%, which is noise (same code);
  projected portal score 0.6118.
expected_effect:
  S:
    pct: -1
    confidence: low
  M:
    pct: 0
    confidence: high
  L:
    pct: 0
    confidence: high
resources_sm100a:
  regs_per_thread: 31
  smem_per_cta_bytes: 0
  threads_per_cta: 256
  ctas_per_sm: 8
  bytes_in_flight_per_sm: 65536
  launches_per_call: 1
knobs:
  ROWS: 16
  THREADS: 256
  PF_MAX_TOKENS: 300
  S_MAX_TOKENS: 600
  PF_DISTANCE: "num_SMs x 8 CTAs (one resident wave), 8 KB per prefetch"
dispatch:
  - max_tokens: 300
    kernel: qk_rms_kernel<2>
    meta:
      loads: "x evict_first, w nc, L2 bulk prefetch one wave ahead"
  - max_tokens: 600
    kernel: qk_rms_kernel<1>
    meta:
      loads: "x evict_first, w nc (parent r6 S path)"
  - max_tokens: 1000000000
    kernel: qk_rms_kernel<0>
    meta:
      loads: "default (parent r6 large path = r5)"
runs_on:
  H200:
    runs: true
    representative: false
    note: "128-bit fallback; the L2 is 50 MB, so prefetch and dirty-flush effects differ"
  A100:
    runs: true
    representative: false
    note: "no bulk prefetch on sm_80 (compiled out); correctness only"
  L40S:
    runs: true
    representative: false
risks:
  - "The expected gain (about 2% on two 256-token workloads, under 1% on the portal after shrinkage) is below the portal's 5% noise; one submission cannot resolve it."
  - "The fixed-cost decomposition comes from a rented B200 at unlocked clocks. At the portal's 1.5 GHz SM lock the per-SM issue costs are a little larger."
  - "If the dirty-flush write-back reading is right, the S band is already close to its floor (launch span + first DRAM latency + doubled read-phase traffic). Further S effort should then go elsewhere."
measure_first:
  - H200
compliance:
  single_stream: true
  no_cross_call_state: true
  fp32_math: true
  no_threads_or_fork: true
  no_precompiled_binaries: true

