I combined the two parents into one dispatcher, `r9-xo-spf-l2mode-disp`. It passed all 16 workloads on the rented B200, and the projected portal score is **0.6121**, against 0.6099 for the current best (r6-s-ldef-nc-disp). Against r6 on the same GPU, the bands moved S −1.6%, M −0.7%, L −0.5%.

Each band roughly matches its better parent, but within noise rather than clearly:
- **S (B·S ≤ 600), from r8-s-pf256-disp:** −1.6% here, against −1.9% for that parent. This is within noise. The two 256-token sizes improved by only 1.3–1.8% here, against 0.9–4.8% in the parent's own test.
- **M and L (B·S > 600), from r8-ldg256-l2mode-disp:** −0.7% and −0.5% here, against −1.2% and −1.2% for that parent. The code is the same, so I read the gap as run-to-run noise. The individual workloads ranged from −2.4% to +0.5%.

All of these changes are about 1–2%, below the portal's roughly 5% noise, so one portal submission cannot confirm them. I ran a single test as asked and did not repeat it.

The sm_100a compile shows the intended 256-bit loads and stores in every kernel (`LDG.256`, `STG.256`), 28 registers on the small-input paths and 31 on the others, with no shared memory and no spills.

### Rationale
Both parent paths are copied unchanged into one `kernel.cu`, as two kernel templates with no PyTorch headers. Dispatch happens on the host from the token count only: up to 300 tokens, up to 600, up to 1536, and above (fallback when `n_rows % 32 ≠ 0`). The only other change is that `num_sms` now goes to the launcher, because the small-input path needs it for the prefetch distance.

